```{r setup, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "",
                      message = FALSE, warning = FALSE)
options(width = 100)
```

*One of this package's seven data sources — one of the four online services. `vignette("data-sources")` is the overview and puts it in context.*

The Province of British Columbia runs the
[BC Address Geocoder](https://geocoder.api.gov.bc.ca/), a public, keyless
service over the province's own site and parcel registries. It is the highest
quality address source this package can reach, and it covers one province.

`bc_geocode()` is the binding, `"bc"` is a `geocode()` tier, and `bc_validate()`
re-asks it about an answer you already have.

```{r connect}
library(cangeocode)
library(dplyr)

con <- nar_connection()
```

## What this adds to the package

**A second opinion from an authority, which nothing else here provides.**
`geocode()` cannot tell you whether its own answer is right — every offline tier
reads NAR, so a NAR error is invisible from the inside. BC's geocoder is a
provincial address authority built on parcels and a site registry, not a
compilation of what others supplied, and where it and NAR disagree **BC's answer
is the more reliable of the two**. That is what `bc_validate()` is for.

It also adds coverage, though less than the quality would suggest. As a tier it
places BC addresses the offline tiers miss; but the two sources plausibly share
upstream data, so it is not independent of NAR and much of what NAR is missing
in BC it is missing too.

And it is the source of the only calibration the package has of what
`uncertainty_m = 0` is actually worth. `uncertainty_m` measures error *this
package* adds relative to NAR's point, and says nothing about NAR's own error;
BC is the one place that second quantity has been measured at all — see
`vignette("source-nar")`.

## Licence

Every response carries its own copyright notice and licence link:

> Copyright © 2024 Province of British Columbia

under the **Open Government Licence – British Columbia**. That composes with the
Open Government Licence covering NAR, so BC coordinates can be mixed into a
result without further conditions — unlike the ODbL data behind `osm_geocode()`,
which is why that one is not a tier.

## Using it

```{r geocode}
bc_geocode("525 Superior St, Victoria, BC") |>
  select(match_method, uncertainty_m, bc_score, bc_precision, bc_descriptor,
         bc_accuracy, lon, lat)
```

As a tier, and as a validator:

```{r tier}
g <- geocode(c("525 Superior St, Victoria, BC", "800 Robson St, Vancouver, BC"),
             method = c("nar", "bc"), con = con)

bc_validate(g) |>
  select(input, match_method, bc_match_method, bc_precision, bc_dist_m)
```

`bc_dist_m` is how far BC's answer sits from the one already obtained, and it is
the column to sort on when looking for suspect rows. Note the second row: it was
placed by the `"bc"` tier in the first place, so validating it re-asks the source
that already answered and the distance is zero by construction. Validation only
says something about rows an *offline* tier placed.

## What to watch out for

### The service always answers

Feed it something that does not exist and it does not fail — it returns the
centre of the locality:

```{r nonsense}
bc_geocode("1234 Nonexistentzzz Rd, Victoria, BC") |>
  select(match_method, bc_score, bc_precision, bc_address, bc_faults)
```

So the presence of a result says nothing. `matchPrecision` is the field that
decides what was actually resolved, and `match_method` is derived from it:
`bc_site` and `bc_civic` are addresses, `bc_block` is interpolated along a
block, and `bc_street` and `bc_locality` are answers about a *place* rather than
an address. `min_score` additionally rejects matches the service itself scored
poorly, and `bc_faults` says why it did.

### Which point on the property you get — and which one you asked for

BC holds several different points for one address, and `locationDescriptor`
selects among them. Their definitions are the service's own:

| descriptor | what it is |
| --- | --- |
| `parcelPoint` | a position known to be inside the parcel, usually the centroid |
| `rooftopPoint` | the roof of the house or building |
| `frontDoorPoint` | the front door or main entrance |
| `accessPoint` | on the curb or road edge, in the middle of the driveway or access lane |
| `routingPoint` | on the road centreline, directly in front of the access point |
| `any` | *(the default)* whatever main location it holds, falling back to the access point when it holds none |

```{r descriptors}
one <- "525 Superior St, Victoria, BC"

lapply(c("any", "rooftopPoint", "accessPoint", "routingPoint"), function(d) {
  bc_geocode(one, locationDescriptor = d) |>
    transmute(asked = d, bc_descriptor, bc_accuracy, lon, lat)
}) |> bind_rows()
```

**Asking does not mean getting**, which is why `bc_descriptor` reports what
actually came back. The service does not go looking for the kind of point you
named; it returns the main location it happens to hold. Over 400 NAR BC building
points (`data-raw/probe_bc.R`), `frontDoorPoint`, `rooftopPoint` and
`parcelPoint` each returned a point **identical to `any` on 100% of addresses**
— the main location was a parcel point for 359 of the 400 and a rooftop for 19,
and no request changed that. Only `accessPoint` and `routingPoint` are genuinely
different points.

### The default is the one closest to NAR

The NAR User Guide says a building representative point "may not correspond
exactly to the physical center of the building structure itself" and may be the
road access point or the driveway — which describes BC's `accessPoint` almost
word for word, and suggests the package should be asking for that one. Measured,
it should not. Distance from NAR's own building point, on the 378 of 400
addresses BC resolved cleanly under every descriptor:

| requested | p25 | p50 | p75 | p90 | within 10 m | within 25 m |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `any` / `parcelPoint` / `frontDoorPoint` / `rooftopPoint` | 1.6 m | **20.2 m** | 50.7 m | 99.1 m | **38%** | **57%** |
| `accessPoint` | 16.6 m | 28.9 m | 52.9 m | 98.7 m | 11% | 43% |
| `routingPoint` | 19.1 m | 31.6 m | 54.7 m | 99.7 m | 6% | 36% |

Paired per address, `accessPoint` is a median 5.3 m *further* from NAR than the
default and `routingPoint` 7.7 m further. So the package's default is already
the right one, and `accessPoint` is what to ask for when the road-side position
is what you actually want — routing, service delivery — rather than the
building.

The per-address picture is more interesting than the aggregate, though. The
default is the closest of the three on only **58%** of addresses; `accessPoint`
wins on 29% and `routingPoint` on 13%. NAR's building points are not
consistently one kind of point or the other — they are a mixture, which is
exactly what the User Guide's hedge describes. There is no single BC point that
NAR is trying to be.

Note also the p25 column: the default agrees with NAR to within 1.6 m on a
quarter of addresses. That is not two sources independently arriving at the same
place — it is the same underlying record surfacing twice.

### `uncertainty_m` for the `bc_*` tiers is not measured

It is the one set of numbers in this package that was not derived from data. BC
publishes `locationPositionalAccuracy` as the categorical `high` / `medium` /
`low` / `coarse` and no distance at all, so the metres attached to `bc_site`,
`bc_civic` and the rest are the service's precision semantics translated into
deliberately pessimistic order-of-magnitude figures. Treat them as a ranking
that is safe to filter on, not as an error bar comparable to the NAR tiers'.

`bc_accuracy` carries the categorical straight through, and it is the more
honest field of the two.

### It covers British Columbia only

`bc_validate()` skips rows outside BC rather than sending them, because the
service would answer about a BC place of the same name.

```{r disconnect}
DBI::dbDisconnect(con)
```

## Where the measurements live

```{r notes, eval = FALSE}
file.show(system.file("notes", "geocoding-status.md", package = "cangeocode"))
```

`data-raw/probe_bc.R` reproduces the reference-point comparison above; the
geocoding status note carries the NAR-versus-BC distance distributions and what
they do and do not prove.
